WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.904
On October 2nd, 2022, Brazilians voted 
for their next president

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:07.374
and most of the votes went 
to two frontrunners.

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:10.744
One was current president and far-right leader. 
Jair Bolsonaro.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:20.720
And the other was Brazil's former president,
the left-wing politician

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.823
Luiz Inácio da Silva, 
also known as Lula.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.196
For months leading up to the election

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.198
Lula polled very well against Bolsonaro

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.035
and was expected to win comfortably 
in the first round.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:38.171
But when the results came in, 
Lula's lead was small.

00:00:38.605 --> 00:00:40.807
As of this video, he and Bolsonaro 

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.709
are set to face each other 
in a runoff.

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:46.246
Lula is Brazil's most famous 
and popular politician.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.381
At one point during his presidency,

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.683
his approval rating 
was nearly 90%.

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.453
But Brazilian politics has changed

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:57.323
and the story of Lula's career is 
a good way to see exactly how.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.064
Lula's left wing politics

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:07.500
are rooted in his days as a factory worker 
and a union leader.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.470
That's where he began his career 
as a politician.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:13.173
During Brazil's military dictatorship

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.175
Lula led massive strikes and protests.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.710
By the 1980s, he had helped organize 

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.880
thousands of trade union supporters 
into a political party.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.483
The Workers Party, or the PT.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:27.020
Lula's coalition drew mainly from 
Brazil's political left.

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.556
It included the working class

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:32.625
low income people, left-leaning
Catholic voters

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.961
Afro-Brazilians and Indigenous people.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:38.598
This is a pretty unique arrangement
of forces.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.634
In my opinion, it's one of the things that 
allowed the Worker's Party

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.837
to grow and become so strong.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:47.874
In 1989, Lula ran as his party's candidate 
for president.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:51.511
He made it to the second round and lost 
by only 4 million votes.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:55.381
Then he lost again in 1994 
and again in 1998.

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.084
His base was growing, 
but not yet enough 

00:01:58.084 --> 00:02:00.019
for him to win a presidential election.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:04.757
During much of this time

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.293
Brazil was experiencing 
an economic crisis.

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.697
At one point, inflation was so 
out of control

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:12.699
that the country switched 
its new currency

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.767
5 separate times 
in 7 years.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:17.570
And about a fifth of the country
was in poverty.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:21.508
In the late 1990s, Brazil's center-right 
led government

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:23.977
passed a series of dramatic 
economic reforms.

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:27.647
They brought inflation down 
by establishing a stable currency

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.782
and they brought down poverty.

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:32.986
But their recovery also left 
much of the country behind...

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.455
including many in Lula's coalition.

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:47.100
But in 2002, when Lula ran 
for president a fourth time

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.369
he tried something a little bit different.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.842
In the 2002 race, Lula brought 
several members

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.977
of Brazil's conservative and 
center-right parties 

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.078
into his campaign.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.215
Including prominent businessman,
José Alencar

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:03.583
as his running mate.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.486
By including them, Lula 
expanded his coalition

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:09.322
attracting the support of 
center-right voters

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:11.424
like business owners and bankers.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:14.961
Lula's this guy that he can 
go to a board meeting

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:18.998
at a major bank or to a poor favela

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.802
and speak in a way that people
will relate to him.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.004
This new kind of coalition

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.707
led to Lula winning his first
presidential election.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.350
In Lula's first term, Brazil's economy 
grew rapidly

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.019
mostly because of a booming 
trade partnership with China.

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:43.323
He benefited from a magical 
economic moment.

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:46.359
He used this cash flow to finance 
a new social welfare

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:49.195
and cash transfer program:
Bolsa Familia

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:50.697
which gave Brazilians a stipend

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.799
for ensuring their children 
were attending school

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:54.667
and up to date on their vaccines.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:59.872
It was a way to give people money 
for their immediate needs

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:03.943
while getting them to put 
their kids on the path

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:07.880
that would eventually help them 
escape that cycle of poverty.

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.250
This program, along with other reforms

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.051
like increasing the minimum wage

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.053
also grew his coalition even further.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.657
And more Brazilians voted for 
left and center-left politicians

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:18.958
politicians across the country.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:20.460
The economy picked up

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.963
and then we had this 
massive period of growth.

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:26.499
Lula became an unstoppable force.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:29.002
By the end of his first term in office

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:31.537
Lula's approval rating reached over 60%.

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:35.308
And even though his administration
began to attract accusations

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:36.643
of bribery and corruption...

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:40.146
Lula's unprecedented popularity 
and coalition building

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:42.482
led to a reelection victory in 2006.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.952
By the end of Lula's second term in office

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.254
GDP was the highest in Brazil's history.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:51.257
And so was the president's approval rating.

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:53.993
OBAMA: The most popular politician on earth.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.400
In 2009, Lula picked a fellow 
Worker's Party member

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.268
Dilma Rousseff, 
as his successor.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.671
She inherited his coalition 

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:07.073
and easily won the presidency 
in 2010.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.309
Early in her administration

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:11.377
global demand for commodities fell

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.112
which led to a recession in Brazil...

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.748
and her approach to managing the economy 

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.985
cost her some support from the 
center-right business community.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.954
Her administration also faced 
accusations of corruption

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:23.756
which cost her even more support.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:26.025
It didn't help that she was 
not a good politician.

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:31.564
She did not negotiate with Congress 
in a very skillful way.

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:35.868
And the economic guidance of 
her administration 

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.203
was very flawed.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:40.106
And then in 2014, something happened

00:05:40.106 --> 00:05:42.408
that would lead to the entire coalition's collapse.

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:44.477
A government investigation

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:46.446
found that many Workers Party officials 

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:47.814
were involved in a corrupt scheme

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:50.016
with Brazil's state owned oil company

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:51.150
including Rousseff...

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:54.120
whose approval rating tanked and in 2016

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.488
she was removed from office.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:06:00.059
Lula was also implicated for 
allegedly taking bribes.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.663
He was convicted in 2017 
and sent to prison.

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:07.367
But unlike Rousseff, 
he remained popular.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:11.537
For a lot of people, despite 
Lula going to jail

00:06:11.537 --> 00:06:12.705
they still love him.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.474
For one, a lot of people say

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:16.342
"Oh, they all rob us."

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:19.345
"At least this guy was giving us 
back something."

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:22.448
But with no one there to hold 
the left coalition together.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:25.418
The opportunity emerged for
an unusual politician

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:27.387
to start building his own from the right.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:32.825
Throughout Rousseff's impeachment

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.461
retired military officer Jair Bolsonaro

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:38.097
was laying the groundwork for 
his chance at the presidency

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.133
in the 2018 election.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.902
Bolsonaro's core supporters 
were evangelicals

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:46.139
farmers, businessmen,
and anti-abortion voters.

00:06:46.873 --> 00:06:48.908
He was the candidate of the far-right

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.511
but he also gained the support 
of the center-right

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:53.146
and even some leftist voters

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:55.214
who are disillusioned with the Workers Party

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:56.883
creating a unique coalition.

00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.019
Meanwhile, Lula's former coalition

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:01.921
was split between several candidates

00:07:01.921 --> 00:07:04.323
helping propel Bolsonaro 
to an easy victory...

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:07.393
and signaling a new political moment 
in the country.

00:07:07.794 --> 00:07:11.597
What Bolsanaro did in 2018 was 
unlike what we had ever seen.

00:07:11.864 --> 00:07:14.767
It was a real shift.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:18.771
The hard-right was replacing 
the moderate-right...

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:21.674
at the center of the political arena.

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:25.311
In office, Bolsonaro oversaw 
further destruction of the Amazon

00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:28.247
extreme hunger and Brazil being 
one of the worst

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:31.350
impacted by COVID with over 680,000 deaths.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.187
Brazil's GDP dipped back down

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:37.523
and he also took a confrontational stance
with Brazilian democracy

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:40.526
trying to control the media and 
undermining the Supreme Court.

00:07:41.227 --> 00:07:45.264
Due to these factors, his popularity 
never exceeded 35%.

00:07:47.166 --> 00:07:51.070
In 2019, Brazil's Supreme Court 
released Lula from prison

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.306
and later annulled his convictions

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:55.975
enabling him to run again in 2022.

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:09.422
Lula also positioned himself

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.557
as a pro-democracy candidate 
in the race.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.533
He worked to assemble another 
strong and diverse coalition

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:22.468
from across the political spectrum.

00:08:22.902 --> 00:08:27.840
Lula has made a front that 
maybe cannot get broader.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.943
Eight former presidential candidates 
from left and right

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:33.212
a big part of the business community.

00:08:33.479 --> 00:08:36.816
Lula even courted evangelicals 
by campaigning at churches.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.820
At the same time, Bolsonaro
enacted social reform programs

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.322
to court low-income and 
working class Brazilians

00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:45.591
who are usually aligned with Lula.

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:47.760
But as the 2022 election approached

00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:50.963
Lula's strategy seemed to be working 
more than Bolsonaro's.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:54.400
The polls were spot-on in regards to Lula.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.004
Lula finished the first round with 
around 48% of the vote.

00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:00.640
But they were dead wrong 
about Bolsonaro.

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:04.076
Lula still won the first round,

00:09:04.076 --> 00:09:07.380
but Bolsonaro outperformed every poll 
leading up to the election.

00:09:07.914 --> 00:09:10.316
And the bigger surprise 
was in Brazil's Congress

00:09:10.316 --> 00:09:12.852
where the right wing candidates 
aligned with Bolsonaro

00:09:12.852 --> 00:09:14.020
won the majority.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:17.356
So even if Lula goes on 
to win the presidency...

00:09:17.657 --> 00:09:20.826
he'll face a stronger opposition 
than he ever has before.

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.329
Lula has made a lot of commitments.

00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:24.964
It remains to be seen 

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.133
how he will be able to implement that.

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.336
This is a force that has to 
be reckoned with.

00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:33.940
It is part of Brazilian mainstream politics

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.743
and will continue like that for years to come.

